{smcl}
{* *! sobol_mata 0.4.2 23oct2026}{...}
{title:Title}

{pstd}{bf:sobol} {hline 2} Generate Sobol sequence coordinates with optional LMS scrambling

{title:Syntax}

{p 8 16 2}{cmd:sobol} {it:newvarlist} [{cmd:,} {opt n(#)} {opt start(#)} {opt scramble} {opt seed(#)}]

{title:Description}

{pstd}
Creates one double-precision variable for each coordinate, in the order listed.
The implementation uses pure Mata, the first 1,024 dimensions of the Joe-Kuo
D(6) direction-number set, 32-bit integer numerators, and Gray-code ordering.
It generates points in [0,1). At index zero the point is the origin without
scrambling, and the digital shift with scrambling.
It does not use or modify Stata's random-number state.

{pstd}
This is a development release. This command creates points. For the package's
separate S1/ST sensitivity functions, see {help mf_sobol_sensitivity}.
Python and compiled plugins are not required to use the command.
Source uses version 16.0; execution has been verified on Stata/MP 16.1 for macOS.
Other releases and operating systems have not yet been tested.

{title:Options}

{phang}{opt n(#)} specifies the number of observations. If omitted (or zero),
the current number is used. An empty dataset requires a positive n().
If observations already exist, n() must equal their number; existing variables
are retained. Values are always stored as double, regardless of set type.
The command limits n() to 2,147,483,647; available memory and Stata variable
limits may impose smaller limits.

{phang}{opt start(#)} specifies the zero-based sequence index of the first
generated point; default zero. It must be a nonnegative integer and start()+n()
must not exceed 4,294,967,296. This option supports continuation and indexed
access. It is not a recommendation to discard initial points.

{phang}{opt scramble} applies a linear matrix scramble (LMS) followed by a
digital shift. It requires {opt seed(#)}. Omitting both options returns the
same unscrambled sequence as version 0.1.0.

{phang}{opt seed(#)} is an integer from 0 through 4,294,967,295. It requires
{opt scramble}. Random bits are generated by a private MT19937 with the 2002
init_genrand initialization. The same seed and sequence indices reproduce
the same points independently of Stata's RNG type, seed, or state.
The same numeric seed in another library need not produce the same scramble.

{title:Examples}

{pstd}Download the repository and keep every file in its ado folder together,
including the five .mata implementation files. Add that folder to the search path:
{phang2}{cmd:adopath ++ "/your/path/SOBOL-Stata/ado"}
{phang2}{cmd:which sobol}
{pstd}This path setting applies to the current session. Update the complete folder
and restart Stata when changing versions. The examples below assume this setup.
Use a disposable dataset; {cmd:clear} removes the data in memory.

{pstd}An empty dataset and a complete 1,024-point net in three dimensions:
{phang2}{cmd:clear}
{phang2}{cmd:sobol u1-u3, n(1024)}
{phang2}{cmd:list u1-u3 in 1/8}

{pstd}The next 1,024 points, as separate variables in the same dataset:
{phang2}{cmd:sobol v1-v3, start(1024)}

{pstd}A reproducible scrambled net and its continuation:
{phang2}{cmd:sobol r1-r3, scramble seed(20261023)}
{phang2}{cmd:sobol s1-s3, start(1024) scramble seed(20261023)}

{pstd}For a Mata matrix or a stateful stream, see {help mf_sobol}.

{title:Numerical use}

{pstd}
Prefer complete prefixes of length 2^m. An arbitrary point count, arbitrary
skipping, or thinning need not preserve the balance properties of a complete
Sobol net. Multiple blocks can be concatenated to recover the same prefix.

{pstd}
Unscrambled output deliberately includes the origin; scrambled coordinates
can also be zero. Transformations singular at zero require an explicit endpoint
strategy; the command neither drops points nor clips values. The optional
midpoint mapping U:+2^(-33) puts all
coordinates in (0,1), but changes the quadrature grid.

{pstd}
For randomized QMC error assessment, compute one estimate per separate
scramble and use the standard deviation of those estimates divided by sqrt(R).
Do not treat points within a net as independent observations. One option is
32 scrambles and an approximate t interval; coverage is not guaranteed for all
integrands. This measures numerical integration error, not parameter sampling
uncertainty. A finite 32-bit grid can also contribute integration error.

{pstd}
Use double storage. Converting coordinates to float can round distinct points
together and can round values close to one to exactly one.

{pstd}
The command writes in blocks of at most 4,096 rows and restores the input
dataset on failure. Dataset preservation itself may require temporary storage.

{title:Stored results}

{pstd}{cmd:r(N)}, {cmd:r(dimension)}, {cmd:r(start)}, {cmd:r(next)},
{cmd:r(bits)}, {cmd:r(seed)} (missing if unscrambled); macros {cmd:r(varlist)},
{cmd:r(version)}, {cmd:r(directions)}, {cmd:r(order)}, {cmd:r(scramble)}
(none or LMS+shift), and {cmd:r(prng)}. Each generated variable also has
sobol_dimension, sobol_start, sobol_bits, sobol_version, sobol_scramble,
sobol_seed and sobol_prng characteristics.

{title:References}

{pstd}Joe, S., and F. Y. Kuo. 2008. Constructing Sobol sequences with better
two-dimensional projections. SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 30: 2635-2654.

{pstd}{browse "https://web.maths.unsw.edu.au/~fkuo/sobol/":Joe-Kuo direction numbers and algorithm notes}.
Direction data are distributed under their BSD-style license, reproduced in
the generated source and JOE_KUO_LICENSE.txt at the repository root.

{pstd}Owen, A. B. On dropping the first Sobol' point.
{browse "https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.08051":arXiv:2008.08051}.

